1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,252
Your house is not an asset?

2
00:00:02,852 --> 00:00:06,185
That's from a book with over thirty two million copies sold.

3
00:00:06,785 --> 00:00:10,940
But which of its six lessons actually work, and which could leave you broke?

4
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:20,338
Here's Rich Dad Poor Dad in about eight minutes. The six lessons, the one picture that explains the whole book, and the parts you should ignore.

5
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:23,451
First, the two dads.

6
00:00:25,451 --> 00:00:33,876
Robert Kiyosaki writes that he grew up with two father figures. His own dad was highly educated, and his advice was simple.

7
00:00:34,476 --> 00:00:37,368
Study hard, get good grades, and find a safe job.

8
00:00:37,968 --> 00:00:44,286
His best friend's dad barely finished school, but he built businesses, and his advice was very different.

9
00:00:44,886 --> 00:00:47,790
Learn how money works, and make it work for you.

10
00:00:48,390 --> 00:00:51,471
When something cost too much, poor dad had one answer.

11
00:00:52,071 --> 00:00:53,151
I can't afford it.

12
00:00:53,751 --> 00:00:55,913
Rich dad asked a question instead.

13
00:00:56,513 --> 00:00:58,008
How can I afford it?

14
00:00:58,608 --> 00:01:02,243
One sentence shuts your brain off. The other switches it on.

15
00:01:02,843 --> 00:01:10,689
That habit, asking how instead of saying I can't, runs through the whole book. Keep it in mind for every lesson that follows.

16
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,062
Lesson one. The rich don't work for money.

17
00:01:17,062 --> 00:01:19,264
Great work this month. Here's your paycheck!

18
00:01:19,864 --> 00:01:30,009
Most people get a paycheck, spend it, and wait for the next one. They get a raise, and their spending rises right along with it. Kiyosaki calls this the rat race.

19
00:01:30,609 --> 00:01:36,960
He says two feelings keep you running. Fear of not paying the bills, and desire for the next upgrade.

20
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:48,454
That's why people with good salaries can still be one missed paycheck away from trouble. Earning more doesn't fix it on its own, if every raise just becomes a bigger lifestyle.

21
00:01:49,054 --> 00:01:53,229
The way out is to make money work for you, instead of only working for money.

22
00:01:55,033 --> 00:01:59,190
Lesson two. Learn the one rule of money they don't teach at school.

23
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:09,254
This is the most important idea in the book. An asset puts money in your pocket. A liability takes money out of your pocket.

24
00:02:09,854 --> 00:02:20,077
So a new car feels like an asset, but it costs you fuel, insurance and repairs every month, and it loses value. By his rule, it's a liability.

25
00:02:20,677 --> 00:02:26,648
Here's a quick test. If you stopped working tomorrow, what would still pay you? That's your asset column.

26
00:02:27,248 --> 00:02:33,634
Now the picture that explains everything. The poor earn a salary, and it goes straight to living costs.

27
00:02:34,234 --> 00:02:42,888
The middle class earn more, but borrow to buy things that look like assets. A bigger house, a new car. The payments eat every raise.

28
00:02:43,488 --> 00:02:49,156
The rich buy assets first. The assets pay them, and that income pays for the luxuries.

29
00:02:49,756 --> 00:02:55,193
By that logic, you're financially free when your assets earn more each month than you spend.

30
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,410
Lesson three. Mind your own business.

31
00:03:01,410 --> 00:03:06,652
Your job is your employer's business. Your business, he says, is your asset column.

32
00:03:07,252 --> 00:03:17,020
He doesn't say quit. Keep the day job, but put your spare money and time into assets. Stocks, real estate, a small business, or things you create.

33
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:26,276
You don't need much to start. Even a small automatic amount into an index fund each month puts the first thing in your asset column.

34
00:03:26,876 --> 00:03:32,209
And here's the rule worth stealing. Buy luxuries last, and let your assets pay for them.

35
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:37,640
Lesson four. Taxes, and the power of corporations.

36
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:43,286
An employee earns, gets taxed, and then spends what's left.

37
00:03:43,886 --> 00:03:51,014
Kiyosaki points out that a business can earn, pay its genuine business costs, and then get taxed on what's left.

38
00:03:51,614 --> 00:04:02,800
Think equipment, software, or a vehicle genuinely used for the business. Not your holiday. Mixing those up is exactly how people get into trouble with the tax office.

39
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:15,897
The rules differ a lot from country to country, so talk to an accountant before you try anything. But the lesson stands. For many people, tax is their biggest bill, so learn how it works.

40
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:20,041
Lesson five. The rich invent money.

41
00:04:22,041 --> 00:04:29,363
This one is about financial intelligence. Learning enough to spot opportunities other people walk right past.

42
00:04:29,963 --> 00:04:39,533
He says financial intelligence comes from four areas. Accounting, so you can read the numbers. Investing. Understanding markets. And the law.

43
00:04:40,133 --> 00:04:49,706
But notice the trap. Every opportunity comes with risk, and the book tells lots of exciting deal stories, and very few about deals that go wrong.

44
00:04:50,306 --> 00:04:56,749
So the real lesson is this. Learn first, start small, and never bet money you can't afford to lose.

45
00:04:58,567 --> 00:05:01,513
Lesson six. Work to learn, don't work for money.

46
00:05:03,513 --> 00:05:07,278
Pick jobs for what they teach you, not only for what they pay.

47
00:05:07,878 --> 00:05:16,615
He singles out a few skills that pay for life. Selling and communicating. Managing people and money. And understanding investing.

48
00:05:17,215 --> 00:05:22,606
That might mean less pay for a while. Think of it as tuition that somebody else pays for.

49
00:05:23,206 --> 00:05:30,258
A narrow specialist is easy to replace. Someone who can sell, lead and invest can build almost anything.

50
00:05:32,067 --> 00:05:34,989
Now, the honest part. What the book gets wrong.

51
00:05:36,989 --> 00:05:47,094
Critics, including the rival author John T. Reed, doubt that rich dad was ever a real person. So read it as a fable, not a biography.

52
00:05:47,694 --> 00:06:01,393
It's light on risk. Borrowing to buy property multiplies losses as fast as gains. And in twenty twelve, Kiyosaki's own company, Rich Global, filed for bankruptcy after losing a lawsuit.

53
00:06:01,992 --> 00:06:12,712
And your house? It doesn't pay you every month, so by his definition it's not an asset. But paying it off builds equity, and you need somewhere to live. It's not that simple.

54
00:06:13,312 --> 00:06:22,031
He also barely talks about the simplest asset of all. Low cost index funds that anyone can buy with almost no expertise.

55
00:06:22,631 --> 00:06:33,700
And while he's right that school skips money, his dismissive tone about education doesn't fit most careers. Doctors, engineers and nurses still need their degrees.

56
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:42,257
So keep the big idea, and leave the hype. An asset puts money in your pocket. A liability takes it out.

57
00:06:42,857 --> 00:06:53,854
Make money work for you. Know an asset from a liability. Build your own asset column. Learn how tax works. Grow your financial intelligence. And work to learn.

58
00:06:54,454 --> 00:07:04,759
Your one job tonight. Take a sheet of paper, draw two columns, and list your assets and liabilities by his definition. That's the whole book, on one page.

59
00:07:05,359 --> 00:07:06,931
I'll see you in the next one.
